+### Atomic staging/publication and read ownership (DI001.c)
+1. Capture the current bound-node revision and concrete request/stream owner **before the first await**. For inspection, bind ownership to that exact in-flight attempt in the existing inspections slot; deleting/replacing it on attach, Stop, release or newer inspection invalidates its result. `finally` may delete only its own slot. `null` result and error callbacks require the same guard as non-null results. Do not let an old read erase a new context or an old `onInactive` remove a newer service.
+2. For streams, callbacks must be qualified by exact service plus binding as well as current socket. Check before reporting errors, clearing service, adopting a snapshot or expanding child rows. Buffered event processing stays behind successful snapshot publication. Stop/release cancels old service work. For inspections, service attachment at any point invalidates the read, even if the new service is not yet ready; the present one-time `isReady()` check is insufficient. New node binding invalidates old work: the collaboration store observes bindingRevision, invalidates its pending read/Stop handles, retires its exact services and releases its owned contexts/selection. It must not reuse an old-node service merely because hostRunId is equal. Extend its existing release mechanics; do not wipe another store's activity data or introduce a global freshness framework.
+3. Once snapshot tree IDs are known, capture **all indexed child activity content revisions synchronously before workspace resolution or projection fetch**. For already-known children in a read-only inspection, capture revisions at inspection start (before the root-view fetch), carrying them into staging; new child IDs are captured when the returned tree is validated, before their member fetch. This prevents pre-view-fetch activity changes from being accepted under a newer revision too. Keep these values; never reread at return/commit as an expected revision. Build every candidate conversation/status/input/activity offscreen. No mutation of old AgentContext/state/config/activity during fetch or candidate construction.
+4. `publish` rechecks read ownership, preflights **all** retained run-ID/address/context associations and any adoption conditions first; then invokes `commitActivities()` once. The existing activity store checks all revisions/duplicates and stages every replacement before publishing any run. Its SR034 terminal-native retention/normal100-item window remains the only retention policy. Only after successful commit may adoption swap old contexts' state/config, preserve local composer identity, publish root reference, update errors/selection/expansion. This is one synchronous no-await section. Preflight must make adoption nonthrowing for valid candidates; no identity validation deferred until after commit.
+5. On stale owner or any revision/identity conflict, discard the whole candidate; old conversations, state/config objects, activities/revisions, selection, expansion and root reference stay unchanged. Existing stream recovery/error path may request a fresh snapshot; explicit inspection reports the conflict and can be requested afresh. Do not mask the conflict by recapturing revisions or retrying the same candidate. No partial commit, destructive clear, post-commit rollback/reinsert or shadow cache. Guarding activities alone does not guard context replacement.
+6. Success Stop must settle old cards **before** its fresh inspection captures revisions; the final empty native history replacement then retains actual stopped/completed/failed cards. Cold empty state still constructs only actual saved records. Live new work may use new operation identity and must not relabel the old one.
+
